{"step_index":646,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nShowing lines 1-126 of 126\n1: ---\n2: name: btrfs\n3: description: >\n4:   Btrfs filesystem administration: subvolumes, snapshots, rollback, balance,\n5:   scrub, RAID profiles, compression, quotas, send/receive backups, and\n6:   troubleshooting.\n7:   MUST consult when installing, configuring, or troubleshooting btrfs.\n8: triggerPhrases:\n9:   - \"btrfs\"\n10:   - \"Btrfs\"\n11:   - \"btrfs snapshot\"\n12:   - \"btrfs subvolume\"\n13:   - \"btrfs balance\"\n14:   - \"btrfs scrub\"\n15:   - \"copy-on-write filesystem\"\n16:   - \"CoW filesystem\"\n17:   - \"btrfs-progs\"\n18:   - \"mkfs.btrfs\"\n19:   - \"snapper\"\n20:   - \"btrbk\"\n21: globs:\n22:   - \"**/fstab\"\n23:   - \"**/etc/fstab\"\n24: last_verified: \"unverified\"\n25: ---\n26: \n27: ## Identity\n28: \n29: | Property | Value |\n30: |----------|-------|\n31: | **Type** | Kernel built-in filesystem (no loadable module needed on most distros) |\n32: | **CLI tool** | `btrfs` (subcommands: `filesystem`, `subvolume`, `balance`, `scrub`, `device`, `quota`, `qgroup`, `check`, `defragment`, `rescue`) |\n33: | **Distro install** | `apt install btrfs-progs` / `dnf install btrfs-progs` |\n34: | **Create** | `mkfs.btrfs` |\n35: | **Kernel version matters** | Significant stability improvements landed between 4.x and 6.x; older kernels have known bugs with RAID-5/6 and balance |\n36: \n37: ## Quick Start\n38: \n39: ```bash\n40: sudo apt install btrfs-progs\n41: sudo mkfs.btrfs -L mydata /dev/sdX1\n42: sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/mydata\n43: btrfs filesystem show /mnt/mydata\n44: btrfs device stats /mnt/mydata\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ## Key Operations\n48: \n49: | Task | Command |\n50: |------|---------|\n51: | Show filesystems | `btrfs filesystem show` |\n52: | Filesystem usage (detailed) | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` |\n53: | List subvolumes | `btrfs subvolume list /mount` |\n54: | Create subvolume | `btrfs subvolume create /mount/name` |\n55: | Delete subvolume | `btrfs subvolume delete /mount/name` |\n56: | Create read-write snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot /mount/src /mount/dst` |\n57: | Create read-only snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot -r /mount/src /mount/dst` |\n58: | Rollback: delete current, rename snapshot | `btrfs subvolume delete /mount/@; mv /mount/@snapshot /mount/@` |\n59: | Rollback: recreate subvolume from snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot /mount/@snapshot /mount/@` |\n60: | Balance start (all chunks) | `btrfs balance start /mount` |\n61: | Balance start (metadata only) | `btrfs balance start -mconvert=raid1 /mount` |\n62: | Balance status | `btrfs balance status /mount` |\n63: | Balance cancel | `btrfs balance cancel /mount` |\n64: | Scrub start | `btrfs scrub start /mount` |\n65: | Scrub status | `btrfs scrub status /mount` |\n66: | Scrub cancel | `btrfs scrub cancel /mount` |\n67: | Check filesystem (read-only) | `btrfs check /dev/sdX` (unmounted) |\n68: | Defragment file or directory | `btrfs filesystem defragment -r /mount` |\n69: | Resize (grow) | `btrfs filesystem resize max /mount` |\n70: | Resize (shrink) | `btrfs filesystem resize -10G /mount` |\n71: | Add device | `btrfs device add /dev/sdY /mount` |\n72: | Remove device | `btrfs device remove /dev/sdY /mount` |\n73: | Device read/write error stats | `btrfs device stats /mount` |\n74: | Enable quotas | `btrfs quota enable /mount` |\n75: | Show qgroup usage | `btrfs qgroup show -reF /mount` |\n76: \n77: ## Expected State\n78: \n79: - `btrfs device stats /mount` → all counters are `0` (any non-zero value is a read/write error)\n80: - `btrfs scrub status /mount` → `Status: finished` with `0 errors`\n81: - `btrfs balance status /mount` → `No balance found on '/mount'` when idle\n82: - `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` → Data and metadata ratios show reasonable allocation (metadata should not be >80% full)\n83: \n84: ## Health Checks\n85: \n86: 1. `btrfs device stats /mount` → all counters `0`; any non-zero value means hardware errors, act immediately\n87: 2. `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` → check both Data and Metadata sections; metadata ENOSPC is the most common silent failure\n88: 3. `btrfs scrub status /mount` → verify last scrub completed with no errors; schedule scrubs monthly via systemd timer or cron\n89: 4. `btrfs subvolume list /mount` → audit snapshot count; accumulation fills disk without appearing in normal `df` output\n90: \n91: ## Common Failures\n92: \n93: | Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n94: |---------|-------|-----|\n95: | `no space left on device` but `df` shows free space | Metadata chunks exhausted while data chunks exist | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` — run `btrfs balance start -dusage=50 /mount` to rebalance underused chunks |\n96: | RAID-1 on data but single on metadata | Dangerous default when adding a second disk; metadata is not redundant | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` — check metadata profile; run `btrfs balance start -mconvert=raid1 /mount` |\n97: | Filesystem mounts read-only | Encountered an error it cannot self-correct | Check `dmesg | grep -i btrfs`; run `btrfs check /dev/sdX` on unmounted device to assess damage |\n98: | Balance fails with ENOSPC mid-run | Not enough free space to relocate all chunks | Use `-dusage=<N>` filter to relocate only partially-used chunks first; free up space before retrying |\n99: | Qgroup show reports inflated usage | Qgroups track snapshot references — shared data counted in each | Shared data is expected; use `btrfs qgroup show -reF` to see exclusive vs shared bytes |\n100: | Snapshot accumulation fills disk | Old snapshots reference deleted data, preventing space reclaim | `btrfs subvolume list /mount | grep snapshot` — delete old snapshots with `btrfs subvolume delete` |\n101: | `btrfs check --repair` made it worse | `--repair` is not safe for general use and can corrupt data | Never run `--repair` without consulting upstream; use `rescue` subcommands instead |\n102: | Filesystem errors after kernel upgrade | Older on-disk format or regression | Check `dmesg`; downgrade kernel or use `btrfs rescue` tools; check known issues for your kernel version |\n103: \n104: ## Pain Points\n105: \n106: - **ENOSPC due to metadata/data imbalance**: The most common Btrfs \"gotcha\". Btrfs allocates space in chunks — if metadata chunks fill up, writes fail even when data chunks are available and `df` still shows free space. Fix: `btrfs balance start -dusage=50 /mount` to reclaim partially-empty chunks. This is not a bug — it is the expected behavior when chunk allocation becomes imbalanced.\n107: - **Mixed RAID profiles between data and metadata**: Adding a second disk does not automatically mirror metadata. A system can have `RAID1` data but `single` metadata, meaning metadata loss on one disk is fatal. Always explicitly set both with `-d` and `-m` flags.\n108: - **Snapshots are instant but they accumulate**: Snapshot creation is O(1) and free. Deletion requires reference counting and can be slow. Unreferenced snapshots hold on to deleted data indefinitely — disk usage only decreases after snapshot deletion completes.\n109: - **`btrfs check` is slow and `--repair` is risky**: Unlike `fsck.ext4`, Btrfs check does not have a safe repair mode. `--repair` can corrupt a filesystem that `check` reports as damaged. Prefer `btrfs rescue` subcommands (`zero-log`, `fix-device-size`) for targeted fixes.\n110: - **Btrfs RAID-5/6 has known parity issues**: As of kernels through 6.x, Btrfs RAID-5 and RAID-6 have unresolved write-hole bugs. Do not use RAID-5/6 for data you care about. Use RAID-1 or RAID-10 instead.\n111: - **Send/receive for backups**: `btrfs send` / `btrfs receive` is efficient for incremental backups using read-only snapshots as base references. Tools like `snapper` and `btrbk` automate the snapshot lifecycle and send/receive workflow — prefer them over manual scripting.\n112: - **CoW fragmentation on databases and VMs**: Copy-on-write writes new data to new locations, which fragments sequential files over time. Use `nodatacow` mount option (or `chattr +C`) for VM disk images, database files, and other large randomly-written files. `nodatacow` also disables checksums for those files.\n113: \n114: ## See Also\n115: \n116: - **ext4** — Traditional Linux filesystem without CoW overhead; use when you need maximum compatibility and don't need snapshots\n117: - **xfs** — High-performance filesystem for large sequential I/O; use for databases and media workloads where CoW fragmentation is a concern\n118: - **zfs** — Full storage stack with pooling and replication; use when you need enterprise-grade checksums and send/receive without kernel version sensitivity\n119: - **lvm** — Logical volume manager; provides snapshot and resize capabilities at the block layer beneath btrfs\n120: \n121: ## References\n122: \n123: See `references/` for:\n124: - `btrfs-options.md` — mkfs and mount options organized by category\n125: - `common-patterns.md` — filesystem setup, subvolume layouts, snapshots, rollback, backup, RAID, and compression\n126: - `docs.md` — official documentation and community links"}
{"step_index":647,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nShowing lines 1-213 of 213\n1: ---\n2: name: incus\n3: description: >\n4:   Incus system containers and VMs: instance/profile YAML, disk devices,\n5:   native storage pools and volumes, Btrfs subvolumes, lifecycle and isolation.\n6:   Consult before creating or changing an Incus instance, profile, disk,\n7:   storage volume, root binding or provisioning helper.\n8: triggerPhrases:\n9:   - \"Incus\"\n10:   - \"Incus disk\"\n11:   - \"Incus profile\"\n12:   - \"Incus storage\"\n13:   - \"container root disk\"\n14:   - \"native storage volume\"\n15: last_verified: \"2026-10-19\"\n16: ---\n17: \n18: # Incus: configuration first, storage-driver ownership\n19: \n20: Project-local skill authored from the official Incus documentation below;\n21: not an imported third-party skill or a container-sandbox wrapper. Documentation\n22: review is not proof that any example has been applied to this host. Check the\n23: installed client/server version and applicable API extensions before using\n24: version-dependent options.\n25: \n26: ## Start with the actual declaration\n27: \n28: An Incus instance is configured through instance properties, `config`,\n29: `devices` and ordered `profiles`. The CLI exposes this as YAML; do not invent\n30: a separate authoritative YAML file on disk. Incus owns its configuration.\n31: \n32: Read both local and expanded instance configuration. Later profiles override\n33: earlier ones; instance-local configuration overrides profiles. Editing a\n34: shared profile affects every instance that uses it. Do not use expanded\n35: configuration as a blind replacement: it flattens inherited settings.\n36: \n37: Read-only infrastructure diagnostics, within the assigned maintenance scope:\n38: \n39: ```sh\n40: incus version\n41: incus config show <instance>\n42: incus config show <instance> --expanded\n43: incus profile show <profile>\n44: incus storage show <pool>\n45: incus storage volume list <pool>\n46: incus storage volume show <pool> container/<instance>\n47: ```\n48: \n49: Confirm the selected remote, project, instance type, native UUID and stopped\n50: or running state. A name alone is not an identity or authorization proof.\n51: Use `--force-local` only when explicitly diagnosing this host's local daemon.\n52: \n53: ## Declare native disks under devices\n54: \n55: Example fragment for a NEW container's root on an existing Btrfs pool named\n56: `3tched` (not a migration instruction for an existing container):\n57: \n58: ```yaml\n59: devices:\n60:   root:\n61:     type: disk\n62:     pool: 3tched\n63:     path: /\n64: ```\n65: \n66: Incus creates the instance's root storage volume at instance creation. Its\n67: Btrfs driver uses a subvolume for a container filesystem volume. Do not\n68: manually create a per-container `.img`, GPT, `losetup` mapping or filesystem\n69: as an assumed prerequisite for this native root declaration.\n70: \n71: Additional managed filesystem volumes are separate objects. Create the\n72: custom volume through the supported control plane first; its device then\n73: names the pool, volume and guest mount path:\n74: \n75: ```yaml\n76: devices:\n77:   data:\n78:     type: disk\n79:     pool: 3tched\n80:     source: identity-data\n81:     path: /data\n82: ```\n83: \n84: This references an existing custom filesystem volume named `identity-data`;\n85: it does not create that volume. Custom volumes persist independently of the\n86: instance until explicitly deleted. Do not casually share identity-private\n87: volumes. A device with a host-path `source` and no pool is a host-backed\n88: mount, not an Incus-managed custom volume.\n89: \n90: For standalone Incus administration, the native commands are\n91: `incus storage volume create <pool> <volume>` (filesystem by default),\n92: `incus storage volume attach <pool> <volume> <instance> <device> <guest-path>`\n93: and `incus config device add <instance> <device> disk pool=<pool> source=<volume> path=<guest-path>`.\n94: These are reference syntax, NOT permission to bypass this repository's\n95: identity-bound provisioning operations. Do not replay them against an\n96: already provisioned identity without the authorized maintenance contract.\n97: \n98: ## Do not conflate these layers\n99: \n100: | Object | Meaning |\n101: | --- | --- |\n102: | Btrfs pool backing | Existing block device, loop file or Btrfs subvolume; inspect the pool's `source`. |\n103: | Container filesystem volume | Managed filesystem tree; Btrfs driver represents it as a subvolume. No additional loop per volume is needed. |\n104: | Container `type: disk` device | Mount inside the container, not a VM-style emulated partitioned disk. A host block source is mounted, not automatically passed through as guest block hardware. |\n105: | Custom `block` or `iso` volume | Attachable to VMs, not containers. Do not switch instance type to work around this restriction. |\n106: | Btrfs VM disk | Incus uses a backing file because Btrfs does not natively provide block volumes. This is not the container-volume model. |\n107: | Btrfs seeding/sprouting | Separate block-device filesystem operation; a subvolume is not a writable block member for `btrfs device add`. |\n108: \n109: A loop backing an entire existing Incus pool does not imply a loop for each\n110: container. Never detach an existing pool backing to satisfy a statement that\n111: container subvolumes need no loops. Do not create new loop-backed pools or\n112: per-container raw images unless that distinct layout is explicitly requested.\n113: \n114: An Incus root declaration alone does NOT implement an external immutable\n115: seed plus writable Btrfs member, a GPT two-partition upgrade replica, or a\n116: read-only overlay. Name any conflict between such requirements and native\n117: filesystem volumes before changing the design. Do not silently replace\n118: seeding with copied roots, snapshots, or an OverlayFS provisioning fallback.\n119: Use the `btrfs` skill for actual seed, membership and send/receive mechanics;\n120: Incus owns its managed-volume lifecycle.\n121: \n122: ## Preserve ownership, lifecycle and admission\n123: \n124: - Begin repository operations in the assigned identity's mounted 3tchedFS.\n125:   Discover declared operations and arguments there. Preserve principal,\n126:   session, grants and Cozo identity-to-storage binding. Missing admission is\n127:   not permission to use the local Incus socket or edit Cozo directly.\n128: - Inspect current native configuration and the provisioning owner before\n129:   changing an existing root. Changing the `pool` key is not a storage migration.\n130:   Preserve native UUID, session/key binding, mount ownership and receipts.\n131: - Keep containers unprivileged, their UID/GID mapping, storage ioctl denials\n132:   and existing device allowlist. Do not grant guest block administration,\n133:   nesting, unrestricted host paths or daemon sockets to make storage work.\n134: - Explicit creation may establish approved storage once. Login, boot, start,\n135:   stop, restart, logout and recovery must not allocate new identity leaves\n136:   or add/remove Btrfs members. Restore/verify existing storage instead.\n137: - Do not mutate Incus-owned storage directories or live overlay lower layers\n138:   behind the daemon. Host storage maintenance must use its supported owner,\n139:   exact device/FSID/mount checks and explicit authorized scope.\n140: - Before any receive, verify the intended mounted destination and its FSID;\n141:   an existing directory is insufficient. Never receive into a fallback path\n142:   on the host root filesystem when the intended mount is missing.\n143: - Distinguish source configuration, accepted operation, native state and\n144:   deployed readiness. Report the operation receipt and authoritative result;\n145:   no claim of success from writing YAML alone. Do not auto-start containers,\n146:   change service managers or run test suites merely to install this skill.\n147: \n148: ## 3tched Ubuntu provisioning contract\n149: \n150: - Use Ubuntu 26.04 LTS (Resolute Raccoon) inside the container. This does not\n151:   change the host's distribution or service manager.\n152: - Reuse the initialized 3tched Ubuntu seed contents, including when the owner\n153:   explicitly selects different backing storage. A generic Ubuntu root archive\n154:   may initialize a new seed only; never use a generic image as a later\n155:   container-root fallback. Never modify a sealed seed with retained dependents.\n156: - Compose each root from that seed through the existing host storage owner.\n157:   Preserve existing 3tched integrations and network declarations; install only\n158:   demonstrated container runtime dependencies.\n159: - Keep persistent CozoDB data on the approved identity-owned writable storage.\n160:   Do not force a separate Cozo subvolume through `btrfs device add`: that\n161:   operation applies only when the selected design actually adds a block member.\n162:   An Incus filesystem subvolume is not a block-device operand. Require the\n163:   exact approved device before adding it; do not infer permission to allocate\n164:   a raw image, loop, LV or replacement disk.\n165: - For an explicitly approved partitioned seed image, a host `losetup\n166:   --find --show --partscan IMAGE` mapping exposes partition devices such as\n167:   `/dev/loopNp1` and `/dev/loopNp2`; separate loop allocations per partition\n168:   are not inherently required. Finalize and unmount the root filesystem before\n169:   setting `btrfstune -S 1` on partition 1 only. Check the existing SEEDING flag\n170:   and skip an already sealed base; never retune on login/start/reboot. The flag\n171:   persists in the image, while kernel mappings must be restored after reboot.\n172:   Protect only the base partition read-only, not the whole image when partition\n173:   2 must remain writable. Never set SEEDING on the upgrade receive filesystem.\n174: - Distinguish an immutable base partition from a writable upgrade receiver.\n175:   Keep the receiver filesystem/parent writable for `btrfs receive`, while\n176:   preserving received snapshots and common incremental baselines read-only.\n177:   Receive a new version into the parent, not over an existing `@upgrade`;\n178:   publish it only through the host-owned overlay lifecycle, without modifying\n179:   an in-use lower tree. Track each container's receiver and baseline explicitly;\n180:   do not infer a shared mutable receiver from a shared image/template name.\n181: - When the owner explicitly requests a raw leaf image under the Cozo store,\n182:   first locate the live store through supported metadata or open-path evidence;\n183:   do not create a duplicate database or read private records to find its path.\n184:   Put image files outside RocksDB `data`, use a private directory and exclusive\n185:   creation, and retain the exact size/inode/operation result. A created image\n186:   does not establish Btrfs membership or migrate Cozo data into that image.\n187: - Distinguish direct regular-file rejection from a provisioning-policy denial.\n188:   Installed btrfs-progs may reject a regular `.img` with `is not a block device`;\n189:   report that real result rather than claiming completion or silently creating\n190:   a loop/device-mapper alternative against the owner's instruction. Full\n191:   allocation does not change a regular file's type: if a nonsparse retry is\n192:   explicitly requested, verify allocated bytes (disable compression on the\n193:   empty file before writing zeros), and do not repeat that allocation after\n194:   the same type rejection.\n195: - Reconcile matching creation receipts and verify the retained storage binding\n196:   on repeats. Normal lifecycle operations must not repeat device-add, format\n197:   storage or recreate roots. Interrupted intent requires reconciliation, not\n198:   an automatic new allocation.\n199: - Reuse existing identity, authorization and policy services. Do not build\n200:   parallel services, mint new credentials, broaden grants or edit Cozo\n201:   directly as part of OS provisioning. Verify the mounted filesystem and\n202:   authoritative binding before claiming persistence is configured.\n203: \n204: ## Official references\n205: \n206: Consult the version matching the installed daemon where behavior differs.\n207: These upstream pages were reviewed for this skill on 2026-10-19:\n208: \n209: - [Instance configuration](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/explanation/instance_config/)\n210: - [Profiles and inheritance](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/profiles/)\n211: - [Disk devices](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/reference/devices_disk/)\n212: - [Btrfs storage driver](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/reference/storage_btrfs/)\n213: - [Storage volume creation, attachment and content-type restrictions](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/howto/storage_volumes/)"}
